{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792606005,"nonce":"5eWjV5cQ9fF5cwK+wMFcqi+1pys=","message":"Implemented shared native startup placement through zrtl_native::build. LLVM-enabled zynode, zypy, zylua and zynml builds group Linux input sections .text.startup and .text.startup.* into one executable output section before ordinary text, using an augmenting linker-script fragment. The constructor arrays are not reordered. Non-Linux targets keep their original linker layout. ZYNTAX_KEEP_LINKER_LAYOUT is a tracked build-time opt-out for custom layouts and matched-source measurements. Script paths use separate -Xlinker arguments, so spaces and commas do not become compiler-driver argument separators. No IR, tiering, runtime cache format, GC or ownership policy changed.\n\nExact-source/feature-matched release controls were built with the opt-out set; the grouped binaries were built from the same files and Cargo.lock with it unset. Source hashes matched local and isolated NUC trees. Builds completed before samples; frozen binaries were hashed before and after. The original tar transfer preserved mtimes older than a previous host SDK compilation and caused one stale-artifact build failure; extracting without preserving mtimes rebuilt the SDK correctly. All final controls/grouped binaries and SDK tests use the refreshed, verified source. Both builds use Rust 1.101.0-nightly c36f14571, LLVM 21.1.8, RUSTFLAGS=-L /home/raikage/llvmshim, release profile, offline/locked Cargo, on the i5-1250P NUC.\n\nELF checks on all four binaries prove identical sequences of all 402 constructors, identical shared-library and version requirements, and identical dynamic symbol names/bindings/visibility (addresses may move). PIE, BIND_NOW, non-executable stack and absence of writable-executable LOAD segments are retained. No GLIBC_ABI_DT_RELR dependency or RELR link flag was added. Constructor-entry code pages, ordinary -\u003e grouped: zynode 482 -\u003e 89; zypy 473 -\u003e 88; zylua 475 -\u003e 88; zynml 489 -\u003e 88. These are code-layout footprints, not RSS claims. Analysis: /private/tmp/startup-layout-abi.json and /home/raikage/startup-layout-abi.json.\n\nControlled process measurements: 336 samples, CPUs 0-7, nine randomized startup samples per language/variant/tier-selection/case, five kernel process guards per combination. Tiny startup cases suppress tier-up/OSR; controls are --version or backend. Kernel guards use normal thresholds/OSR with Cranelift or LLVM selected; these are total process timings, not steady-state throughput or proof every function reached LLVM. Every checksum/status matched. Pre-sample checks found zero compiler jobs, \u003c=1.345 busy cores and \u003c=70C; no throttle counters changed. Checks were pre-sample, not continuous monitoring. No Cargo between samples and no local wall-time claims.\n\nMedian wall ms, ordinary -\u003e grouped (Cranelift selection / LLVM selection):\n- zynode launch control: 3.550 -\u003e 3.364 / 3.407 -\u003e 3.253; tiny program 4.699 -\u003e 4.597 / 4.836 -\u003e 4.733.\n- zypy launch control: 3.443 -\u003e 3.447 / 3.526 -\u003e 3.364; tiny program 9.389 -\u003e 9.381 / 9.724 -\u003e 9.671.\n- zylua launch control: 3.553 -\u003e 3.430 / 3.400 -\u003e 3.359; tiny program 11.205 -\u003e 10.614 / 10.884 -\u003e 10.825.\nTiny-program minor faults (Cranelift / LLVM selection): TS 1015 -\u003e 1021 / 1040 -\u003e 1035; Python 1650 -\u003e 1593 / 1705 -\u003e 1630; Lua 1904 -\u003e 1821 / 1948 -\u003e 1902. Small timing differences are within noise in some modes; do not claim a universal frontend speedup. Parsing/lowering still dominates Python's tiny program. The reliable result is reduced constructor code spread without a deployment ABI change, with modest process-start improvements.\n\nKernel process guards, ordinary -\u003e grouped wall ms (Cranelift / LLVM selection):\n- TS fib(33): 15.384 -\u003e 15.547 / 15.902 -\u003e 15.674; branchy(20M): 122.408 -\u003e 121.371 / 113.800 -\u003e 114.304.\n- Python fib(33): 11.131 -\u003e 10.769 / 11.267 -\u003e 10.913; branchy(20M): 76.344 -\u003e 75.637 / 71.338 -\u003e 72.165.\n- Lua fib(33): 31.765 -\u003e 31.654 / 28.960 -\u003e 29.850; branchy(20M): 64.700 -\u003e 64.639 / 70.147 -\u003e 70.560.\nThe five-sample Lua LLVM fib median was 3.1% higher, so it was checked with 21 additional randomized samples per binary: ordinary 31.230 ms, grouped 31.200 ms (CPU 40.103 -\u003e 40.187 ms), not a reproducible slowdown. Preserve both reports; no throughput improvement is claimed. Report /private/tmp/startup-layout-bench.json (NUC /home/raikage/startup-layout-bench/results.json), follow-up /private/tmp/startup-layout-lua-followup.json and same filename under /home/raikage.\n\nFrozen binary SHA256 controls / grouped:\n- zynode: 86d5433eba8452f4f9fdbb3cb55de572bae10d5358b8b2e4854e44e0ccf65628 / 9bd86eef413f8ff413a9a25709d681f8ffcc1b69d6f99e650e6cb337841ec011\n- zypy: 1a61021789b342274ee80c04d3554df398409d6aef1abe4921fe5b7d07da5509 / 35c038eea2f960ee3b78ee3a187575ba281b5cd405e51b9b47be6f18b40b597a\n- zylua: e5bedbca1d44d97e1f76af795298fcb76106eee3430ba7648d87b22608c6c5e2 / 1cbffc118e1ed000d4fcabc6cf2dab731f358425af61dab55a0400ad14f0c0fa\n- zynml (layout/ABI validation only): 68c5652d253341273e23c6e168098637f7905145d886c9d16027f6fc1819c1bb / 6b16fdcb9ecea7b8e05032eaf24fda8273ea3a5a86a8950eef8e598adfeaf370\nPaths /home/raikage/startup-layout-{control,final}-{zynode,zypy,zylua,zynml}.\n\nValidation: NUC SDK suite 12 passed, including native GCC/bfd linking with garbage collection, PIE/RELRO/NOW, constructor priorities and script paths containing spaces and commas; macOS SDK suite 11 passed (Linux linker fixture correctly excluded). The actual four binaries exercise Rust's default LLVM linker. All four frontend LLVM builds and local all-target checks passed; SDK and TS all-target Clippy passed with warnings denied. Frozen zynode passed all 735 pinned differential conformance comparisons across interpreter, baseline and LLVM. Lua C-module conformance categories passed with compile worker off and on. Existing attribute-derive-macro future warning is unchanged.\n\nPython module categories report 15 pass and one pre-existing failure per worker mode: modules/array_module.py reads the mixed-caller generator argument as None. Both frozen control and grouped binaries fail identically with LLVM off/on. Reproducer/evidence was added to existing issue cd8f6032e388059571047a18754f7a73f9a013166fcd9bc4ad0d8682c9a3488b, now priority:high. No KNOWN_FAILURES entry was added, and the startup change does not resolve that frontend bug. Relevant logs: /home/raikage/startup-layout-{verified-sdk-tests,lua-capi-tests,python-module-tests,conformance}.log; local /private/tmp/startup-layout-{frontends-check,sdk-clippy,ts-clippy,sdk-tests-current}.log. README files were left introductory.","files":null}]}